<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Wellness and Mindfulness – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Wellness and Mindfulness</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Exercise Helps My Body</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Resolve misunderstandings in peer interactions. Concept: Interpret graphical information for classmates.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can help peers understand wellness activities by demonstrating exercises with body language (e.g., shows a stretching exercise while explaining its purpose). Can interpret graphs on exercise benefits by pointing out data and explaining it in simple terms, using familiar extracurricular activities in Panama (e.g., points to the bar on the chart that shows how students' energy improved after practicing baseball or folkloric dance, and explains how the graph shows students' stress levels decreased after participating in track and field or afternoon meditation sessions). Can clarify wellness terms by using both English and familiar language (e.g., describes "mindfulness" in simple terms in both languages).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing images of various Panamanian extracurricular activities related to exercise (e.g., students playing baseball, practicing folkloric dance, or doing track and field). The teacher asks students: "What kind of exercise is this? What benefits does it have for your body?" Students respond in pairs, sharing one or two benefits. Example: "Playing baseball helps strengthen your body and improve your focus."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to explain an exercise and its benefit using simple body language. For example, the teacher performs a simple stretch and says: "This is a hamstring stretch. It helps improve flexibility and prevents injury. I am stretching to maintain my physical health." The teacher also models how to clarify terms if a peer doesn't understand, using simpler words or a familiar language equivalent. For example: "Flexibility means your body can move easily, like being 'flexible' when you do yoga." (Pronunciation focus: 'flexible', 'flexibility').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I show you an exercise, what should you do?" (Expected: Explain its benefits.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If your partner doesn't understand a word like 'resilience,' what can you do?" (Expected: Explain it simply or use another language.) (Pronunciation focus: 'resilience', 'resilient').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content:</b> The teacher presents a simple bar graph titled "How Exercise Impacts Student Well-being" showing two categories: 'Energy Levels' (e.g., 'Improved' vs. 'Unchanged') and 'Stress Levels' (e.g., 'Decreased' vs. 'Increased') after participating in various school activities like "Baseball Practice," "Folkloric Dance," "Track and Field," and "Afternoon Meditation Sessions." The graph uses simple visual representations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A projected bar graph (digital image or printed poster) with clear labels and simple data. The register is informative, and the format is graphical information.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret graphical information for classmates.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is this graph about?" "What main idea does it show?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> The teacher points to a bar (e.g., 'Energy improved after Baseball Practice') and asks: "Which activity shows the most energy boost?" Students identify the activity. "Which activity helps most with stress reduction?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Interpreting graphical information, resolving misunderstandings, clarifying terms. Grammar: Present continuous (ongoing actions), Present simple (general statements), Collocations (energy boost). Vocabulary: wellness, mindfulness, exercise, energy, benefits, strength, flexibility, health, body, mental well-being, routine, practice, meditation, relaxation, nutrition, balance, endurance, resilience, focus, habit, boost, improve, strengthen, relax, meditate, exercise, promote, balance, enhance, maintain, walk, stretch, breathe, focus, hydrate, mental, physical, healthy, strong, flexible, mindful, energized, balanced, calm, peaceful.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Graph Interpretation Sentences:</b> The teacher displays incomplete sentences based on the graph. Students complete them orally or in writing. Example: "According to the graph, after ___________, students' stress levels __________." (e.g., "After track and field, students' stress levels decreased.") "Practicing __________ is helping students ________ their __________." (e.g., "Practicing folkloric dance is helping students boost their energy.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Clarifying Wellness Terms:</b> In pairs, students receive cards with wellness terms (e.g., 'mindfulness', 'endurance', 'hydration'). Partner A picks a card and explains the term in English using simple words. If Partner B struggles, Partner A must clarify, rephrase, or offer a familiar Spanish equivalent (e.g., "Mindfulness is focusing on the present, like 'estar presente' or 'atención plena'"). The teacher circulates, observing the use of simple present/continuous and pronunciation of target vocabulary (e.g., 'mindfulness').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Explain the Benefits:</b> Students return to the graph. In pairs, one student (A) points to a section of the graph and explains its meaning and benefits to their partner (B), using phrases like "This shows that..." or "The data indicates...". Partner B listens and asks for clarification if needed. If B misunderstands, A must re-explain using different words or demonstrating an exercise with body language. (Pronunciation focus: minimal pairs like "He could wear a suit" vs. "He cooed at the baby" can be used subtly by the teacher in feedback if a mispronunciation of a similar sounding word occurs, e.g., confusing 'good' with 'food' when discussing nutrition, although not directly related to 'suit'/'soot' context, the principle of vowel contrast applies to ensure clear communication during mediation).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Students present a section of their "Wellness Blog" project to a small group of classmates (3-4 students). The focus for this lesson is mediation: explaining their chosen wellness routine, interpreting any relevant data or demonstrating an exercise, and clarifying any terms or concepts their peers might not understand.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are encouraged to describe their habits using the present continuous ("I am practicing mindfulness every morning") and present simple ("Mindfulness has many benefits for my mental well-being"). They should share wellness practices ("I exercise three times a week to stay fit and focused") and support varied routines ("Some people like to meditate because it helps them manage stress"). They must be prepared to help peers understand wellness activities by demonstrating exercises with body language (e.g., shows a stretching exercise while explaining its purpose) or by interpreting graphical information they might have included in their blog (e.g., a simple chart showing their progress). They should use familiar language to clarify terms (e.g., 'relaxation' - 'relajación').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Small group presentations and discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students directly explain their personal wellness routines and how exercise helps their body, integrating the concepts of wellness and mindfulness from their blog project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Peer assessment using a simple checklist/rubric and teacher observation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> During the group presentations, each student will use a "Mediation Checklist" to evaluate their peers. The checklist focuses on how well their classmates clarified information, interpreted data, and resolved misunderstandings during their blog explanations. The teacher will also circulate and observe.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (on checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation when presenting blog content.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to interpret simple graphical information (if included in blog).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of body language to explain exercises.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to clarify wellness terms using simple English or familiar language.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially of multisyllabic words (e.g., 'mindfulness', 'flexibility').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary and grammar (Present simple/continuous).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts individually:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one wellness tip from your blog that you successfully explained to your peers?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did you help a classmate understand a difficult term or concept today?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">6-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the mediation process (explaining, clarifying, interpreting) did you find most challenging today, and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can you improve your ability to help others understand health and wellness information in English?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What aspects of the lesson successfully promoted mediation skills? Were students able to interpret graphical information and clarify wellness terms effectively? Did the use of body language aid understanding? What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed to further develop students' mediation abilities, especially regarding resolving misunderstandings in peer interactions?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Wellness and Mindfulness" theme and the "How Exercise Helps My Body" unit. The skills of mediation (explaining, interpreting, clarifying) will be crucial for any future collaborative projects or discussions where students need to communicate complex ideas or resolve linguistic challenges in English. The blog project can be displayed in the classroom or school website to promote a culture of wellness.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>